BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-philosophy-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144745Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Philosophy Competition: First Edition
DESCRIPTION:The AI Philosophy Competition\, organised by philosophers Zacha
 ry Goodsell and Elliott Thornley and funded by Forethought Research\, aims
  to gauge the philosophical capabilities of AI in a way transparent to the
  philosophical community. Entrants submit up to three original essays on a
 ny philosophical topic\; essays must be primarily AI-generated\, with some
  human guidance allowed\, and each entry must include a methodology report
  explaining how the essay was produced. Essays are capped at 6\,000 words 
 each and may not be previously published or under review elsewhere\; entra
 nts consent to online publication and may choose anonymity. Judging is bli
 nd to methodology and authorship and evaluates the quality and originality
  of the philosophical content (style is not graded)\; the judging committe
 e includes David Chalmers\, John Hawthorne\, Herman Cappelen\, Cian Dorr a
 nd other academic philosophers. PRIZES: USD 3\,000 / 2\,000 / 1\,000 for t
 he top three essays\, plus a USD 5\,000 creative methodology pool. Finalis
 ts announced early December 2026\, results end of January 2027. DEADLINE: 
 31 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Zachary Goodsell and Elliott Thornley\, 
 funded by Forethought Research\nLocation: Online submission via OpenReview
  (preferred) or Google Forms\; open internationally.\nAward: USD 3\,000 fi
 rst prize\, USD 2\,000 second\, USD 1\,000 third\, plus a USD 5\,000 pool 
 for creative methodology distributed at the judges' discretion (USD 11\,00
 0 total). No entry fee.\nDuration: Finalists announced early December 2026
 \; results announced end of January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.zacharygood
 sell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.zacharygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission via OpenReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; ope
 n internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:in-development-asterisk-ai-developing-countries-essay-contest-2026@arti
 ficialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T144745Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: In Development x Asterisk Essay Contest: How Will AI Affe
 ct Developing Countries? (USD 10\,000 top prize)
DESCRIPTION:An essay contest run by the development-economics publication I
 n Development and Asterisk Magazine on how transformative AI will affect l
 ow- and middle-income countries\, and how those countries might respond. E
 ssays can focus on one country\, a region or a group of countries\; the to
 pic is open\, with suggested angles including whether AI drives convergenc
 e or divergence between rich and developing countries\, labour-market impa
 cts\, political economy\, the diffusion of health technology\, and philant
 hropic funding from AI companies. Essays should be deeply researched with 
 original analysis\; reporting is encouraged but not required. All selected
  essays are fact-checked and edited before publication. FORMAT: 2\,000 to 
 5\,000 words in English\, previously unpublished (including on personal bl
 ogs or newsletters). Co-authored entries are allowed\, but each person may
  appear on only one entry. AI POLICY: AI may be used for research\, for tr
 anslating an essay written in another language\, and for feedback\, but su
 bmissions containing AI-generated content beyond translation are not accep
 ted. PRIZES: one winner USD 10\,000\, two runners-up USD 5\,000 each\, two
  honourable mentions USD 2\,000 each\, plus a USD 2\,500 African Tech Futu
 res Lab prize for the best essay by an African national. Judges include Cl
 ara Collier (Asterisk)\, Lauren Gilbert (In Development) and Rose Mutiso (
 African Tech Futures Lab). DEADLINE: 31 December 2026\, 11:59 PM Anywhere 
 on Earth\, via the Google Form linked from the announcement. Questions: es
 saycontest@indevelopmentmag.com.\n\nOrganisation: In Development and Aster
 isk Magazine\, with a special prize from the African Tech Futures Lab\nLoc
 ation: Worldwide\, online submission. Authors based in low- and middle-inc
 ome countries particularly encouraged.\nAward: Winner USD 10\,000 plus pub
 lication in Asterisk (print and online) and In Development\; two runners-u
 p USD 5\,000 each plus publication in Asterisk (online) and In Development
 \; two honourable mentions USD 2\,000 each. Separate African Tech Futures 
 Lab prize of USD 2\,500 for the best essay by an African national\, combin
 able with the main prizes. No entry fee stated.\n\nMore: https://indevelop
 mentmag.substack.com/p/how-will-ai-affect-developing-countries?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://indevelopmentmag.substack.com/p/how-will-ai-affect-developing-c
 ountries?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\, online submission. Authors based in low- and middle-in
 come countries particularly encouraged.
END:VEVENT
